sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0243/0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, single-swamp-node (per-Node workers retired), agent-observer-swamp-acts. docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#remove-a-node.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: node:remove
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane P icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Node 12<br/>reached over ssh
  participant P as Fleet Nodes<br/>vpn hub · Metrics
  CLI->>GW: node:remove 12<br/>--force · --offline
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:remove 12 --force`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--force] [--offline] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node, by numeric id
  %%> - `--force` confirms without a prompt and consents to shedding Node 12's roles; required without a terminal and with `--json`
  %%> - `--offline` also allows removal when Node 12 can't be reached
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes Node 12 from Orbit and hands the machine back, reachable over public SSH
  %%> - keeps user data, package managers and unrelated SSH keys on the machine
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Check guards, record<br/>Node 12 removing · task #6202
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - Node 12 has no Instances, Database servers, Schedules, active Routes or operator firewall rules
  %%> - Node 12 is not your own Node, the Gateway Node, the `vpn` Node or the Swamp Node 209
  %%> - a task VM host has no task VMs and no task VM host setting; a task VM guest is removed by its own lifecycle
  %%> - no other change runs for Node 12; the Gateway holds one lock per Node and one for the hub
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the role removal steps for each role, the public SSH rule, and the list of Orbit files and units on Node 12
  %%> - the participants: the hub, the DNS catalog, the Metrics Node and the Cluster Router, with their addresses and SSH host keys
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Node 12 marked `removing`, so hub and DNS renders leave it out
  %%> - task #6202 recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-remove
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - Node 12's addresses and SSH host keys, its managed user and its Orbit SSH block
  %%> - the role removal steps, the files to remove, and each participant's change
  %%> - the `offline` flag; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #6202
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Probe Node 12<br/>@swamp/ssh · new connection
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - opens a new SSH connection over WireGuard and runs `true`
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a shared connection can outlive a stopped sshd, so only a new one proves Node 12 is reachable
  %%> Result:
  %%> - an unreachable Node fails here with `node.unreachable`, unless `--offline` was given
  alt Node 12 is reachable
    opt Node 12 has roles
      SW->>N: Shed each role<br/>shared role remove steps
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - runs the same steps as `node:role:remove` for each role, in the same run
      %%> - removes the role's runtime, certificates and firewall rules; keeps its data and container volumes
      %%> Not included:
      %%> - a child task per role
    end
    SW->>N: Restore public SSH<br/>ufw orbit:public-ssh-recovery
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - adds the public SSH recovery rule while the tunnel still works
    %%> - checks that SSH answers on the public address
    %%> Why:
    %%> - without it, the machine is reachable only through its provider console once the peer goes
    %%> macOS:
    %%> - skipped; Orbit never managed the Mac's firewall
    SW->>N: Remove the Node agent<br/>orbit-agent · exporters · units
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - stops and disables `orbit-agent`, node-exporter, cAdvisor and the PHP-FPM exporter
    %%> - deletes their units and files in `/etc/orbit`, then reloads systemd once
    %%> How:
    %%> - official `@swamp/cfgmgmt/systemd` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file` behaviors over SSH from 209
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - per-Node worker stop, files and token revocation; per-Node workers are retired
    SW->>N: Remove the Orbit SSH block<br/>authorized_keys · marked block
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - deletes only the lines between the Orbit markers
    %%> - keeps every other key, so operator access stays
    %%> Why last:
    %%> - after this step Swamp can no longer log in to Node 12
  else --offline and unreachable
    Note over SW: Leave Node 12 untouched<br/>retained_on_node
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - skips every step on Node 12
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the result lists what stays on the machine: the Node agent, its files and the Orbit SSH block
  end
  SW->>P: Withdraw the hub peer<br/>wg syncconf · drop DNS records
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches the `vpn` Node over `@swamp/ssh` from 209; there is no worker on Node 1
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes Node 12's peer, validates the hub configuration and live-syncs it with `wg syncconf`; other peers keep their tunnels
  %%> - removes Node 12's private names from the DNS catalog and reloads the listener
  %%> Cluster:
  %%> - a Cluster Router drops Node 12 from its LAN rules, through the shared firewall step
  opt a Metrics Node exists
    SW->>P: Drop the Metrics target<br/>validate, reload Prometheus
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes Node 12 from the Prometheus configuration and validates it with `promtool`
    %%> - reloads Prometheus while it runs; no restart
  end
  SW->>P: Check Node 12 is gone<br/>wg show · DNS · Prometheus
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that the hub has no peer for Node 12, and that DNS and Prometheus no longer name it
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the removal only counts when the fleet shows it
  alt every step passes
    P-->>SW: Node 12 absent
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - `removed: true`, with `roles_shed` and `retained_on_node`
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the run's outputs; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Delete Node 12 records<br/>task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12, its role assignments, access grants and SSH host key pins are deleted
    %%> - task #6202 succeeds and lists `roles_shed` and `retained_on_node`
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0
    %%> When:
    %%> - a role removal, the public SSH check, a file removal or a participant change fails, or the budget ends
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code, and which steps finished
    Note over GW: Keep Node 12, mark failed<br/>task failed · rerun is safe
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Node 12 stays, marked failed at that step, with its records
    %%> - task #6202 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> - public SSH is restored before anything else goes, so the machine stays reachable
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same `node:remove` again after fixing the cause; finished steps find nothing left to remove
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #6202
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
